<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berlin Wall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Wall was a guarded barrier built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> from 13 August 1961 to cut off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> and stop East Germans fleeing to the West. It became the leading symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its opening on 9 November 1989 accelerated the collapse of communist regimes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="Berlin Wall" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlinermauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> west of the Oder-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line was divided into four occupation zones, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, the seat of the Allied Control Council, into four sectors, although it lay wholly within the Soviet zone. The inner German border was closed officially in 1952, but the border in Berlin, administered by all four powers, stayed far more open, and Berlin became the main route by which East Germans left for the West. Before the Wall, 3.5 million East Germans, 20% of the population, had left the GDR.</p><p>Emigrants tended to be young and well-educated, which fed official fears of a brain drain. On 15 June 1961 Walter Ulbricht told a press conference that no one had the intention of erecting a wall. A transcript of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> call of 1 August suggests the initiative came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>, though other sources say he was wary of Western reaction and that Ulbricht had pushed for a closure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Construction began on 13 August 1961. The Wall was more than 140 kilometres long, with guard towers and concrete walls, an outer wall and an inner wall, and between them a wide area later known as the death strip. This held anti-vehicle trenches, a strip of raked sand that showed footprints, beds of nails and clear fields of fire. It went through four versions, from a wire fence and concrete block wall in 1961 to the Grenzmauer 75 of 1975 to 1989.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> propaganda presented it as the Anti-Fascist Protection Rampart, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a>'s mayor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willy-brandt">Willy Brandt</a>, called it the Wall of Shame. There were nine official crossings, the best known being Checkpoint Charlie, restricted to Allied personnel and foreigners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>More than 100,000 people attempted to escape, and about 5,000 succeeded. A research group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> confirmed at least 140 deaths, while others estimate well above 200. In 1989 revolutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the Pan-European Picnic set off a peaceful development in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> largely broke. On 9 November 1989 the East German government announced that all GDR citizens could visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a>. Crowds crossed and climbed onto the Wall, and souvenir hunters chipped away pieces of it. Full demolition began on 13 June 1990 and ended in 1994, and German reunification took place on 3 October 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berlin Wall” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West Germany joined NATO in 1955, faced the <b>Berlin Wall</b> from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through Willy Brandt's Ostpolitik in the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From August 1989 East Germans occupied the West German embassy in Prague, and after 3 November thousands left Prague by train for the West; on 9 November the <b>Berlin Wall</b> fell.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>East Germany's Auferstanden aus Ruinen spoke of a single German fatherland, which sat badly with the state after it built the <b>Berlin Wall</b> in 1961, so that on official occasions only its tune was played.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>These themes soon sat badly with the Cold War, above all after the East German government built the <b>Berlin Wall</b> in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>Overnight, East German forces closed the crossings and began building barriers that became the <b>Berlin Wall</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Speaking at the Brandenburg Gate, President Reagan challenged the Soviet leader to open the <b>Berlin Wall</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
